  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nA cold front extending from a cut off low in the Great Lakes \nmoves through during the daytime today. This brings the chance \nfor a strong thunderstorm across the north near the Canadian \nborder this afternoon before the front pushes off to the \nnortheast. Elsewhere, scattered showers and a thunderstorm are \npossible, but overall a drying trend is expected through the day\nbehind the front. Highs generally warm into the 70s to near 80s\nwith sunshine returning.\n\nDespite the front passing, the chance for scattered showers and\nstorms lingers as the cut off low in the Great Lakes takes a \nfew more days to slowly progress eastward through southern \nCanada. So while the overall trend is drier, showers remain \npossible through Tuesday.\n\nCooler temperatures return tonight with the drier airmass in \nplace. Lows range from the mid 50s along the coast, to the upper\n40s through the northern valleys. A stray shower can't ruled \nout during the overnight hours still. Seasonable temps return on\nTuesday again, with highs mainly in the 70s to near 80 degrees.\n\nEarly morning fog, locally dense, is likely.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nUpper-level low finally fully ejects out of the region by \nWednesday. Brief ridging Wed-early Thu should bring tranquil \nconditions with comfortable dew points persisting and seasonable\ntemperatures. As we head deeper into Thursday, southerly flow \nreturns out ahead of the next trough, which will advect a more \nhumid airmass back into the region. \n\nA surface low closes off over Hudson Bay, sending a cold front \nthrough our region Friday night. Guidance indicates \nprecipitation chances Thursday afternoon/evening primarily in \ninterior zones away from the coast. There will be another chance\non Friday with the actual front that looks more widespread as \nof now.\n\nNothing noteworthy for rainfall at the moment, but long range \nmodels do already have a 30 to 50% chance for rainfall exceeding\na half inch. At first glance QPF is scattered, indicating a \nmore convective precip distribution is possible. Should see good\nmoisture return with dew points reaching the upper 60s, which \nwill provide the necessary juice for locally heavy downpours.\n\nFirst stab at next weekend suggests it could be a pleasant one \nwith drying westerly flow behind the cold front, ushering in \ncomfortable dew points in the 40s/50s. It will be a wait and see\nto know for sure though, as some guidance suggests the cold \nfront may get hung up near the Southern New England coast.",
